      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Simple+Minds/Street+Fighting+Years",
      "wiki_content": "One of the most political albums released by the band, with its commemoration of Victor Jara (Street Fighting Years), who was killed by Pinochet in Chile, its commentary on the impact of Margaret Thatcher's poll tax on Scotland (Soul Crying Out), the celebration of the then still imprisoned Nelson Mandela (Mandela Day) and a track about the ongoing \"Troubles\" in the North of Ireland (Belfast Child). <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Simple+Minds/Street+Fighting+Years\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "One of the most political albums released by the band, with its commemoration of Victor Jara (Street Fighting Years), who was killed by Pinochet in Chile, its commentary on the impact of Margaret Thatcher's poll tax on Scotland (Soul Crying Out), the celebration of the then still imprisoned Nelson Mandela (Mandela Day) and a track about the ongoing \"Troubles\" in the North of Ireland (Belfast Child). <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Simple+Minds/Street+Fighting+Years\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Released in 1989, Street Fighting Years is Simple Minds’ eighth studio album and a marked departure from the polished, arena-sized sound of Once Upon a Time. Produced by Trevor Horn and Stephen Lipson, it favors expansive arrangements, atmospheric keyboards, layered guitars, orchestral color, and a more reflective, politically engaged mood. The album’s long-form songs give weight to themes of injustice, solidarity, and historical memory, with “Mandela Day,” “Biko,” and “Belfast Child” standing among its defining statements.\n\nThe album became Simple Minds’ fourth UK number-one record and produced “Belfast Child,” their first UK number-one single. Its grand but sombre approach divided opinion: some critics admired its ambition and emotional depth, while others found the production overly smooth and its political writing too direct. Now represented in the 2026 National Album Day Icons context, Street Fighting Years remains a significant Rock and Pop release—less immediate than its predecessor, but richly atmospheric and central to the band’s socially conscious late-1980s work.",
